<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pucangan inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1041</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Calcutta Stone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pucangan inscription is a stone inscription of 1041 issued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/airlangga">Airlangga</a>, king of Kahuripan in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a>. It recounts the destruction of the court of his predecessor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharmawangsa">Dharmawangsa</a> in 1016 and Airlangga's rise to restore the kingdom. The stone, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a> Stone, was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 19th century and is kept in the Indian Museum, Kolkata.</p></div>
